indicator(
     "CT CPCV Research Lab v1.2 [Pine v6]",
     shorttitle = "CT CPCV Lab v1.2",
     overlay = false,
     max_bars_back = 5000
)


// ============================================================================
// VERSION HISTORY
// ============================================================================
//
// v1.2
// ----
// • Expanded the framework to ten complete candidate strategies.
// • Added Sharpe, Sortino, Calmar, Omega, Ulcer Performance, and Compound
//   Return as selectable training objectives.
// • Added an optional separate training Sharpe filter.
// • Added purged and embargoed CPCV path reconstruction.
// • Added Split Diagnostics and Path Diagnostics tables.
// • Added the Results Guide table.
// • Added the Research Integrity score and grade.
// • Added independent visibility, position, and text-size controls for every
//   table.
// • Added educational tooltips for research-critical settings.
// • Expanded the open-source and research-methodology documentation.
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// CT CPCV RESEARCH LAB
// ============================================================================
//
// PURPOSE
// -------
// This script intentionally displays its results in tables rather than chart overlays because CPCV evaluates research quality, not price action.
//
// Evaluate and compare complete trading strategies using
// Combinatorial Purged Cross-Validation.
//
// This script ranks robustness — not profitability.
//
// A strategy may produce a high historical return and still be fragile.
// Another strategy may produce a lower return while remaining more consistent
// across purged, embargoed, out-of-sample paths.
//
// The purpose of this framework is to help users distinguish between:
//
//   1. A strategy that benefited from one convenient historical sequence.
//
//   2. A strategy that remained comparatively stable across multiple
//      reconstructed out-of-sample histories.
//
// A positive CPCV result does not guarantee future profitability.
//
// It means the strategy survived a more demanding historical validation
// process and may deserve further investigation.
//
// RESEARCH PRINCIPLE
// ------------------
// Good quantitative research attempts to disprove an idea, not merely confirm
// it.
//
// This framework is intentionally designed to make historical strategies fail
// more honestly.
//
// A strategy that survives CPCV has earned further investigation.
//
// It has not earned certainty.
//
// GUIDING RULE
// ------------
// Every line of this script should either:
//
//   1. Perform the research.
//
//   or
//
//   2. Teach the research.
//
// WHAT THIS SCRIPT IS
// -------------------
// An open-source research framework for comparing complete trading strategies
// under identical CPCV conditions.
//
// WHAT THIS SCRIPT IS NOT
// -----------------------
// • It is not a buy/sell recommendation.
// • It is not proof that a strategy has a permanent edge.
// • It is not designed merely to maximize historical return.
// • It does not replace execution testing, slippage analysis, or live trading.
//
// QUICK START
// -----------
// 1. Select a research date range.
// 2. Select the number of chronological CPCV groups.
// 3. Choose the training optimization metric.
// 4. Enable the candidate strategies you want to compare.
// 5. Study the median path.
// 6. Study the lower-tail path.
// 7. Check the positive-path rate.
// 8. Compare return against drawdown.
// 9. Review the Research Integrity panel.
// 10. Do not worship the best split. It is merely one row with confidence.
//
// CANDIDATE OUTPUT STANDARD
// -------------------------
// Every candidate must produce a persistent desired-position series:
//
//   +1.0 = long
//    0.0 = flat
//   -1.0 = short
//
// Users may replace any candidate block with their own causal strategy logic.
//
// Do not use future data, negative historical offsets, or lookahead_on.
//
// ============================================================================

// ============================================================================
// WHY CPCV?
// ============================================================================
//
// A single backtest evaluates one historical sequence. It may accidentally fit
// one bull market, one volatility regime, or one unusually convenient crisis.
//
// CPCV divides history into chronological groups and repeatedly holds out
// combinations of groups as test data.
//
// PURGING
// -------
// Removes training observations near the beginning of a test segment where
// label or holding-period information could overlap the test sample.
//
// EMBARGO
// -------
// Removes training observations immediately after a test segment to reduce
// nearby temporal contamination.
//
// RECONSTRUCTED PATHS
// -------------------
// Test segments are combined into complete out-of-sample paths. The path
// distribution is usually more informative than the best individual split.
//
// This implementation uses k = 2 test groups and supports even N values:
// 4, 6, or 8. Even N allows a valid round-robin perfect-matching path map.
//
// ============================================================================
// PERFORMANCE METRICS ACADEMY
// ============================================================================
//
// SHARPE RATIO
// ------------
// Mean return divided by total return volatility.
//
// Why it remains useful:
// • Widely reported.
// • Easy to compare.
// • Familiar to allocators and researchers.
//
// Why it is insufficient alone:
// • Treats upside and downside volatility equally.
// • Volatility is not the same thing as permanent capital loss.
// • Can be misleading for skewed, fat-tailed, or regime-sensitive returns.
//
// Sharpe is not useless or dead. It is simply an elderly statesman who should
// no longer be permitted to run the entire committee by himself.
//
// SORTINO RATIO
// -------------
// Mean return divided by downside deviation.
//
// Useful when:
// • Upside volatility is desirable.
// • Evaluating directional or trend-following systems.
// • The return distribution is asymmetric.
//
// CALMAR RATIO
// ------------
// Annualized compounded return divided by absolute maximum drawdown.
//
// Useful when:
// • Capital preservation matters.
// • Comparing systematic trading strategies.
// • The user wants return relative to the worst experienced equity loss.
//
// OMEGA RATIO
// -----------
// Sum of positive returns divided by the absolute sum of negative returns,
// using zero as the threshold in this implementation.
//
// Useful when:
// • Returns are non-normal or skewed.
// • The user wants gains compared directly with losses.
//
// ULCER PERFORMANCE
// -----------------
// Annualized compounded return divided by the Ulcer Index.
//
// The Ulcer Index measures the depth and persistence of drawdowns. It rewards
// equity curves that recover quickly rather than merely exhibiting low general
// volatility.
//
// COMPOUND RETURN
// ---------------
// Total compounded return with no risk adjustment.
//
// Useful only when:
// • Risk is evaluated separately.
// • The user consciously wants raw return to dominate model selection.
//
// ============================================================================
// INPUTS
// ============================================================================

string GRP_SAMPLE = "1. Research Sample"

int startTime = input.time(
     timestamp("01 Jan 2015 00:00 +0000"),
     "Start Date",
     group = GRP_SAMPLE,
     tooltip =
          "Beginning of the historical sample used by the CPCV research engine.\n\n" +
          "Bars before this date are ignored.\n\n" +
          "Choose a starting point that includes market conditions relevant to the strategy being studied.\n\n" +
          "A very short sample may produce unstable or misleading results."
)

int endTime = input.time(
     timestamp("31 Dec 2035 23:59 +0000"),
     "End Date",
     group = GRP_SAMPLE,
     tooltip =
          "Final date included in the historical CPCV sample.\n\n" +
          "Bars after this date are ignored.\n\n" +
          "Use this setting to freeze a research period, exclude recent data, or compare results across different historical eras.\n\n" +
          "The End Date must be later than the Start Date."
)

int maxBars = input.int(
     5000,
     "Maximum Stored Bars",
     minval = 500,
     maxval = 9000,
     group = GRP_SAMPLE,
     tooltip =
          "Maximum number of historical observations stored by the research engine.\n\n" +
          "When the limit is reached, the oldest stored bar is removed before a new bar is added.\n\n" +
          "Larger values:\n" +
          "• Preserve more history.\n" +
          "• Increase calculation time.\n" +
          "• Increase memory use.\n\n" +
          "Ten candidate exposures are stored for every included bar.\n\n" +
          "This limit is separate from the Start Date and End Date settings."
)

int annualization = input.int(
     252,
     "Bars Per Year",
     minval = 1,
     group = GRP_SAMPLE,
     tooltip =
          "Number of chart bars treated as one year when annualizing volatility and performance metrics.\n\n" +
          "Common examples:\n" +
          "• Daily US equities: approximately 252.\n" +
          "• Weekly data: approximately 52.\n" +
          "• Monthly data: 12.\n\n" +
          "Intraday values depend on the chart interval and the number of active trading sessions or hours.\n\n" +
          "An incorrect value will distort annualized Sharpe, Sortino, Calmar, volatility scaling, and related metrics."
)


string GRP_CPCV = "2. CPCV Validation"

int groupCount = input.int(
     6,
     "Chronological Groups N",
     options = [4, 6, 8],
     group = GRP_CPCV,
     tooltip =
          "Divides the selected research sample into chronological groups.\n\n" +
          "The script uses two groups as test data in every split.\n\n" +
          "Smaller N:\n" +
          "• Larger individual groups.\n" +
          "• Fewer train/test combinations.\n" +
          "• Fewer reconstructed paths.\n\n" +
          "Larger N:\n" +
          "• Smaller individual groups.\n" +
          "• More train/test combinations.\n" +
          "• More reconstructed paths.\n\n" +
          "N=4: 6 splits and 3 reconstructed paths.\n" +
          "N=6: 15 splits and 5 reconstructed paths.\n" +
          "N=8: 28 splits and 7 reconstructed paths."
)

int purgeBars = input.int(
     5,
     "Purge Bars Before Test Segments",
     minval = 0,
     maxval = 500,
     group = GRP_CPCV,
     tooltip =
          "Removes training observations immediately before each test segment.\n\n" +
          "Purging helps prevent information leakage when a training observation's holding period, label, or outcome window overlaps the beginning of the test sample.\n\n" +
          "For fixed-horizon labels, this value should normally be at least as long as the forward outcome horizon.\n\n" +
          "Too small:\n" +
          "May allow temporal leakage.\n\n" +
          "Too large:\n" +
          "Reduces the amount of available training data."
)


int embargoBars = input.int(
     5,
     "Embargo Bars After Test Segments",
     minval = 0,
     maxval = 500,
     group = GRP_CPCV,
     tooltip =
          "Removes training observations immediately after each test segment.\n\n" +
          "Embargo reduces contamination from observations that occur too close to the held-out test period.\n\n" +
          "This is useful when nearby observations may share market state, holding-period effects, or overlapping information.\n\n" +
          "Too small:\n" +
          "May leave residual temporal dependence.\n\n" +
          "Too large:\n" +
          "Reduces the amount of available training data."
)

int minTrainBars = input.int(
     100,
     "Minimum Training Observations",
     minval = 20,
     group = GRP_CPCV,
     tooltip =
          "Minimum number of valid training observations required before a candidate can be selected.\n\n" +
          "This helps prevent the engine from choosing a strategy based on an unrealistically small training sample.\n\n" +
          "Higher values demand more evidence but may invalidate more splits.\n\n" +
          "Lower values allow more splits but increase the risk of unstable model selection."
)

int minTestBars = input.int(
     30,
     "Minimum Test Observations",
     minval = 10,
     group = GRP_CPCV,
     tooltip =
          "Minimum number of valid out-of-sample observations required for a split result to be accepted.\n\n" +
          "Very small test samples can produce unstable returns, drawdowns, and risk metrics.\n\n" +
          "Higher values improve statistical credibility but may invalidate more test segments."
)


string GRP_EXEC = "3. Return Simulation"

string directionMode = input.string(
     "Long & Short",
     "Allowed Direction",
     options = ["Long Only", "Short Only", "Long & Short"],
     group = GRP_EXEC,
     tooltip =
          "Controls which position directions the candidate strategies may use.\n\n" +
          "Long Only:\n" +
          "Long signals remain long. Short signals are converted to flat.\n\n" +
          "Short Only:\n" +
          "Short signals remain short. Long signals are converted to flat.\n\n" +
          "Long & Short:\n" +
          "Both long and short positions are permitted."
)

float costBps = input.float(
     2.0,
     "Cost Per Unit of Turnover, bps",
     minval = 0.0,
     step = 0.1,
     group = GRP_EXEC,
     tooltip =
          "Applies a trading cost whenever effective exposure changes.\n\n" +
          "One basis point equals 0.01%.\n\n" +
          "Examples:\n" +
          "• Flat to long: 1 unit of turnover.\n" +
          "• Long to flat: 1 unit of turnover.\n" +
          "• Long to short: 2 units of turnover.\n\n" +
          "Higher values penalize high-turnover strategies more heavily.\n\n" +
          "This setting can represent commissions, spread, slippage, and other execution costs."
)

bool useVolTarget = input.bool(
     false,
     "Use Volatility Scaling",
     group = GRP_EXEC,
     tooltip =
          "Scales every candidate strategy's exposure toward a constant annualized volatility target.\n\n" +
          "When estimated market volatility rises, exposure is reduced.\n\n" +
          "When estimated market volatility falls, exposure may increase up to the Maximum Leverage setting.\n\n" +
          "This is a risk-normalization technique. It helps compare strategies under more consistent risk levels rather than identical nominal position sizes.\n\n" +
          "Disabled:\n" +
          "Exposure remains fixed at its normal directional value.\n\n" +
          "Enabled:\n" +
          "Exposure is adjusted using the volatility lookback, target volatility, and leverage cap."
)

int volLength = input.int(
     20,
     "Volatility Lookback",
     minval = 5,
     group = GRP_EXEC,
     tooltip =
          "Number of bars used to estimate realized volatility.\n\n" +
          "Shorter lookbacks:\n" +
          "• React more quickly to changing volatility.\n" +
          "• Produce more frequent leverage adjustments.\n\n" +
          "Longer lookbacks:\n" +
          "• Produce smoother volatility estimates.\n" +
          "• React more slowly to sudden regime changes.\n\n" +
          "Only used when Volatility Scaling is enabled."
)

float targetAnnualVol = input.float(
     15.0,
     "Target Annual Volatility, %",
     minval = 1.0,
     step = 0.5,
     group = GRP_EXEC,
     tooltip =
          "Desired annualized volatility used by the risk-scaling calculation.\n\n" +
          "Illustrative examples:\n" +
          "• 10% = lower risk target.\n" +
          "• 15% = moderate risk target.\n" +
          "• 20% = higher risk target.\n\n" +
          "The engine adjusts exposure toward this target while respecting the Maximum Leverage limit.\n\n" +
          "This is not a profit target.\n\n" +
          "Only used when Volatility Scaling is enabled."
)

float maxLeverage = input.float(
     2.0,
     "Maximum Leverage",
     minval = 0.1,
     maxval = 10.0,
     step = 0.1,
     group = GRP_EXEC,
     tooltip =
          "Caps the exposure produced by volatility scaling.\n\n" +
          "Example:\n" +
          "A value of 2.0 prevents scaled exposure from exceeding two times the normal position size.\n\n" +
          "This prevents extremely low estimated volatility from creating excessive leverage.\n\n" +
          "Only used when Volatility Scaling is enabled."
)

string GRP_SELECT = "4. Model Selection"

string selectionMetric = input.string(
     "Sharpe",
     "Optimization Metric",
     options = [
          "Sharpe",
          "Sortino",
          "Calmar",
          "Omega",
          "Ulcer Performance",
          "Compound Return"
     ],
     group = GRP_SELECT,
     tooltip =
          "Chooses how CPCV ranks candidates using TRAINING data only.\n\n" +
          "Sharpe: Return relative to total volatility.\n\n" +
          "Sortino: Return relative to downside volatility only.\n\n" +
          "Calmar: Annualized return relative to maximum drawdown.\n\n" +
          "Omega: Positive returns relative to negative returns.\n\n" +
          "Ulcer Performance: Annualized return relative to drawdown depth and persistence.\n\n" +
          "Compound Return: Highest raw compounded return without risk adjustment."
)

bool useTrainSharpeFilter = input.bool(
     false,
     "Use Separate Training Sharpe Filter",
     group = GRP_SELECT,
     tooltip =
          "Optional secondary filter.\n\n" +
          "When enabled, candidates must satisfy both the chosen optimization objective and the minimum Sharpe value."
)

float minTrainSharpe = input.float(
     0.0,
     "Minimum Training Sharpe",
     step = 0.1,
     group = GRP_SELECT,
     tooltip =
          "Only used when the Separate Training Sharpe Filter is enabled.\n\n" +
          "Candidates whose training Sharpe falls below this threshold cannot be selected, even if they score highest on the chosen optimization metric.\n\n" +
          "This provides an optional minimum risk-adjusted quality filter."
)

string GRP_EMA = "5. Candidates 1–5 — EMA Crossovers"

bool enable1 = input.bool(true, "Enable 1", group = GRP_EMA, inline = "c1", tooltip = "Includes Candidate 1 in CPCV model selection.\n\nWhen disabled, this candidate is ignored during every training split.")
string name1 = input.string("EMA 5/20 — Fast", "", group = GRP_EMA, inline = "c1", tooltip = "Display name used in the Split Diagnostics table.\n\nChanging the name does not change the strategy logic.")
int fast1 = input.int(5, "Fast", minval = 1, group = GRP_EMA, inline = "p1", tooltip = "Fast exponential moving-average period.\n\nSmaller values react more quickly but may increase noise and turnover.")
int slow1 = input.int(20, "Slow", minval = 2, group = GRP_EMA, inline = "p1", tooltip = "Slow exponential moving-average period.\n\nLarger values produce a slower trend reference and may reduce short-term whipsaws.")

bool enable2 = input.bool(true, "Enable 2", group = GRP_EMA, inline = "c2", tooltip = "Includes Candidate 2 in CPCV model selection.\n\nWhen disabled, this candidate is ignored during every training split.")
string name2 = input.string("EMA 10/30 — Fast/Medium", "", group = GRP_EMA, inline = "c2", tooltip = "Display name used in the Split Diagnostics table.\n\nChanging the name does not change the strategy logic.")
int fast2 = input.int(10, "Fast", minval = 1, group = GRP_EMA, inline = "p2", tooltip = "Fast exponential moving-average period.\n\nSmaller values react more quickly but may increase noise and turnover.")
int slow2 = input.int(30, "Slow", minval = 2, group = GRP_EMA, inline = "p2", tooltip = "Slow exponential moving-average period.\n\nLarger values produce a slower trend reference and may reduce short-term whipsaws.")

